         "label": "module_8",
         "name": "module_8",
         "params": {
          "answer_size": 15,
          "css_class": "dnd-module",
          "headline_lh": "normal",
          "headline_size": 32,
          "items": [
           {
            "answer": "Custom formulation makes sense when you need a formulation that doesn't exist in our catalog, when you want proprietary ownership of the formula, when you're targeting a specific clinical outcome that requires specific dosages, or when you have a defensible insight about an ingredient combination. For most first-time brands, private label is the right starting point.",
            "question": "When does custom formulation make sense instead of private label?"
           },
           {
            "answer": "12 to 16 weeks from concept to production-ready, depending on complexity. The phases: formulation development, ingredient sourcing, stability testing, final formulation lock, manufacturing scale-up, and first production run. Some categories take longer because of stability testing requirements.",
            "question": "What are typical timelines for custom formulation?"
           },
           {
            "answer": "Higher than private label, because we need to amortize R&D, ingredient sourcing, and stability testing investments. Typical custom minimums start in the low thousands of bottles per SKU. We give you specific numbers during the scoping conversation.",
            "question": "What are the minimums for custom formulation?"
           },
           {
            "answer": "You do. Custom formulations are your proprietary IP. We document the formula in a way that you could (in theory) take to another manufacturer, though we hope you won't. The development relationship is transparent: you see the rationale, the testing, and the final spec.",
            "question": "Who owns the custom formula we develop together?"
           },
           {
            "answer": "Yes. We have supplier relationships with most major branded ingredient companies. Branded ingredients add cost but give you marketing leverage (the brand story, the published research). We help you decide where branded ingredients are worth the premium and where they aren't.",
            "question": "Can custom formulations contain branded ingredients (Sensoril, NKO, etc.)?"
           }
          ],
          "max_width": 820,
